    reg {
        name = "Register-config table address";
        desc = "Physical address of the per-channel register-config table in DDR, written once per frame by H264E_DRV_WriteRegs (the value is ctx+0x67c of the channel context). The table is 64-byte aligned (the vendor aligns the address up to 64 and reserves 1 KB: H264E_CreateChn) and holds the picture-level registers (source/reconstruction/reference addresses and strides, slice header bits, picture configuration, ME/ROI/OSD/QP/scaling-matrix words, stream buffer address and ring-pointer addresses); software fills it (H264E_DRV_InitRegConfigParam 0x1db40 at channel creation, H264E_DRV_UpdateRegConfigParam 0x1d4d0 per frame, H264E_DRV_UpdateOsdConfigParam 0x1dae0 on OSD change) and flushes the data cache of the touched ranges (hil_mmb_flush_dcache_byaddr) before START. MODE.regcfg_mode = 1 selects this mode. The table layout is documented in docs/rdl-campaign/vedu-vendor.md, section 6. Register name and field names are the HiSTB drv_venc_reg_r006.h names (neighbor generation, naming only: the offset and the bits listed here were confirmed in the Hi3520D vendor code, the name is inferred). Source: vendor module hi3520D_h264e.ko (H264E_DRV_WriteRegs 0x1bfec, store at +0x1e0), confidence HIGH for offset and role, MEDIUM for the table semantics, word-checked: yes. Not verified on hardware.";

        field {
            name = "addr";
            desc = "Physical address of the register-config table (64-byte aligned). SECOND PASS 2026-11-11 (docs/rdl-campaign/vedu-vendor.md section 13): the table is a PACKED image of registers of the r006 register file; the vendor-written words reproduce the r006 bit layouts of about 80 registers (the registers added to this edition at 0x028-0x14C and 0x200-0x3BC are named from that match, each marked INFERRED). Table map (table offset -> r006 register, MEDIUM unless noted; all offsets hex): 00 SRCYADDR, 04 SRCCADDR, 08 RCNYADDR, 0C RCNCADDR, 10 REFYADDR, 14 REFCADDR, 18 SSTRIDE, 1C RSTRIDE, 20-3C SLCHDRSTRM0-7, 40 PICFG0, 44 PICFG1, 48 PICFG2, 4C PICFG3, 50 and 54 written 0 (PICFG4/5? LOW), 58 VEDIMGSIZE, 5C SLCHDRPARA, 60-6C PTS0-3, 70 ROICFG, 74 ROIQP0, 78 ROIQP1, 7C not written, 80 MECFG0, 84-90 not written, 94 METHR0, 98 METHR1, 9C MERDOCFG, A0 MDCFG, A4 MDCFG0, A8-BC MDCFG1-6, C0-DC ROISIZE0-7, E0-FC ROIPOS0-7, 100 OSDQP_CFG, 104 OSD_QP0, 108 OSD_QP1, 10C VPP_OSD_CFG, 110-124 OSD alpha words, 128/12C OSD QP words (LOW), 130-13C four constants (RC1/QPDELTATHR position guess, LOW), 140-15C VPP_OSD_POS0-7, 160-17C VPP_OSD_SIZE0-7, 180-1BC OSD address/stride (LOW), 1C0-23C SCALE (32 words, High profile only), 240 STRMADDR, 244 SRPTRADDR, 248 SWPTRADDR, 24C STRMBUFLEN, 250 MECFG1, 254-260 MERECTCFG0-3, 264 not written, 268-2B4 MODLAMBDA0-19 (HIGH for position and the 12-bit layout; the 40 default values are a vendor constant: 1,1,1,2,2,3,3,4,5,7,9,11,14,17,22,27,34,43,54,69,86,109,137,173,218,274,345,435,548,691,870,1097,1382,1741,2193,2763,3482,4095,4095,4095; the index of the first value is probably QP 12 (LOW, 40 values = QP 12..51), the vendor packs value[2k] in bits [11:0] and value[2k+1] in bits [23:12] of word k). The table order is NOT the register order (STRM* and MECFG1/MERECTCFG sit at the end), so the hardware must use a fixed, hardware-defined gather order; which words it loads is unknown. Picture type codes in the vendor context: 4 = I picture (no reference address, inter enables 0, PICFG0.ipicture = 1), 5 = P picture (reference address from the reference queue; INFERRED from the branches in UpdateRegConfigParam, MEDIUM). MODE writes per frame: bit 0 = 0, bit 2 = OSD present, bits [4:3] = 0, bit 25 = 1; MODE.vencEn (bit 1 in r006) is never set by the vendor code (the board script writes MODE = 0): INFERRED that it is not needed for the table mode (LOW).";
            sw = w;
            hw = r;
        } addr[31:0];

    } REGCFG_ADDR @ 0x1E0;

    reg {
        name = "QP threshold / chroma QP offsets";
        desc = "Read-modify-write per frame by H264E_DRV_WriteRegs: bits [26:22] and [31:27] both get the value (attribute word & 0x1f). Read back in the per-frame result block: bits [21:16] (H264E_DRV_ReadRegs, result word +0x30). In r006 the register is QPTHR (minQp [5:0], maxQp [13:8], startQp [21:16], chromaQpOff [26:22], secondChromaQpOff [31:27]); the vendor code confirms exactly the two upper fields as written and [21:16] as read, nothing else. The rate-control module writes min/max/start QP through the DDR table, not through this register (RC_ConfigHw only builds a parameter block; LOW). Register name and field names are the HiSTB drv_venc_reg_r006.h names (neighbor generation, naming only: the offset and the bits listed here were confirmed in the Hi3520D vendor code, the name is inferred). Source: vendor module hi3520D_h264e.ko (H264E_DRV_WriteRegs 0x1bfec, H264E_DRV_ReadRegs 0x1c080), confidence HIGH for the bit positions, MEDIUM for the meaning, word-checked: yes. Not verified on hardware.";

        field {
            name = "start_qp";
            desc = "Read per frame into the result block (r006 startQp). BOARD 2026-11-11: reads 0x1C (28) after the first encode runs (QPTHR 0x001C3300) and the hardware wrote slice_qp_delta = se(START_QP - 26) = +2 into the slice header (PROVEN, 40 runs), so this field is the slice QP the hardware encodes and signals; it is not loaded from the table (word 0x130 is not this register). A driver that wants another QP must write this field before START (untested).";
            sw = r;
            hw = w;
        } start_qp[21:16];

        field {
            name = "chroma_qp_off";
            desc = "Written per frame (attribute & 0x1f).";
            sw = rw;
            hw = r;
        } chroma_qp_off[26:22];

        field {
            name = "second_chroma_qp_off";
            desc = "Written per frame with the same value as chroma_qp_off.";
            sw = rw;
            hw = r;
        } second_chroma_qp_off[31:27];

    } QPTHR @ 0x350;

    reg {
        name = "Target bits";
        desc = "Read in the per-frame result block (H264E_DRV_ReadRegs, result word +0x3c, whole word); the result block goes to the rate-control module through the module callback table. r006 name VEDU_TARGETBITS at this offset. Meaning (the rate controller's target bit budget as seen by the hardware) is inferred from the r006 name only. Source: vendor module hi3520D_h264e.ko (H264E_DRV_ReadRegs 0x1c080), confidence HIGH for the access, LOW for the meaning, word-checked: yes. Not verified on hardware.";

        field {
            name = "targetbits";
            desc = "Read once per frame.";
            sw = r;
            hw = w;
        } targetbits[31:0];

    } TARGETBITS @ 0x364;

    reg {
        name = "Picture bits";
        desc = "Read in the per-frame result block (H264E_DRV_ReadRegs, result word +0x38, whole word) and handed to rate control. r006 name VEDU_PICBITS (bits of the encoded picture) at this offset; the meaning is inferred from that name (the stream length itself is computed by software from the stream ring pointers, not from this register). Source: vendor module hi3520D_h264e.ko (H264E_DRV_ReadRegs 0x1c080), confidence HIGH for the access, LOW for the meaning, word-checked: yes. Not verified on hardware.";

        field {
            name = "picbits";
            desc = "Read once per frame.";
            sw = r;
            hw = w;
        } picbits[31:0];

    } PICBITS @ 0x374;

